<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II, Count Palatine of Simmern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1492–1557</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> from 1509 to 1557, ruling a small Wittelsbach territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> together with a share of Sponheim. He introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in his lands and was a patron of learning, supporting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press at Simmern. His son Frederick III inherited the Electoral Palatinate in 1559, when the senior electoral line died out.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-count-palatine-of-simmern.jpg" alt="John II, Count Palatine of Simmern" width="233" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohannIISimmern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
